==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{dercat|ang|gem-pro|ine-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*haitan|t=to command, name}}.

{{rel-top|Cognates}}
Cognate with {{cog|ofs|hēta}}, {{cog|osx|hētan}}, {{cog|goh|heizzan}}, {{cog|non|heita}} ({{cog|sv|heta}}), {{cog|got|𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of {{cog|grc|κίειν|t=put in motion}}, {{cog|la|ciēre|t=rouse, make active}} and {{cog|sq|cys|t=to spur, set in motion}}. {{rel-bottom}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|hātan}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|hātan}}

# to [[call]], [[name]]
# to [[order]], [[command]]
#* '''c. 897''', inscription on the {{w|Alfred Jewel}}
#*: {{quote|ang|''Ælfrēd mec '''hēht''' ġewyrċan.''|{{w|Alfred the Great|Alfred}} '''ordered''' me made.}}

====Conjugation====
{{ang-conj|hātan<s7>}}

This verb has a special passive present form, which is attested as ''hātte'' in the singular and ''hātton'' in the plural. The second person singular is not attested in this form, but is thought to be ''*hāttest''. This passive form was used to give a person's name, functioning like German ''[[heißen]]'', with which it is cognate.

For the past tense, the usual strategies for expressing the passive were used: ''iċ wæs ġehāten'', etc.

